        "title": "Colour in art",
        "text": "A  There are no 'real' colours in nature, only the various wavelengths that make up light, which are absorbed and reflected by all the objects around us. The reflected wavelengths enter the eye, which in turn sends signals to the brain; only then do we 'see' the effect of all these wavelengths on the eye. This white light contains the colours of the rainbow, which can be seen when the rays are separated by a glass prism. Each colour has its own wavelength: violet has the shortest and red the longest. When these colours are combined with nature's pigments, such as the chlorophyll in grass, millions of shades can be created. Painters have reproduced these with the powdered colours of natural or artificial pigments, which are themselves only the colour of the light they reflect.\n\nB  However, before we can properly explore what colour means to the artist, we must ask what we mean by the word 'colour'. This may seem simple enough, but is my 'red' the same as your 'red'? Questions of language are often central to an interpretation of the historical use of colour in art. The Japanese word *awo* can mean 'green', 'blue' or 'dark', depending on the context, and Vietnamese does not distinguish green from blue either. The Aborigines, the indigenous people of Australia, have only two colour terms, white and black, even though they use other colours in their paintings. English has the colour words black, white, red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, pink, grey and brown, and thousands of other 'borrowed' names, such as apricot, avocado, gold and peach.\n\nC  Before the 19th century, when artists were deciding which colour paint to use, they generally had to use 'natural products': that is, organic substances that came from animals and plants. The earliest cave paintings were made with red from iron oxides, black from charcoal and white from chalk and ground bones. Later, Tyrian purple, the imperial colour of ancient Rome, was extracted from a particular shellfish: just under half a kilo of royal purple dye required four million molluscs to be crushed. Cochineal red, discovered by the Aztecs, was made from the female cochineal beetle.\n\nD  Interestingly, however, the blue pigment known as Egyptian frit or Egyptian blue, which has been identified in objects dating from around 2500 BC, is not a naturally occurring product. It was made by the ancient Egyptians, who first had to mix different compounds together and then heat them. The result was the oldest synthetic pigment, a Bronze Age blue. This use of nature's materials shows that ancient Egypt was a civilisation with a real command of chemistry.\n\nE  The cost and quality of the colours were factors that both artist and patron had to bear in mind when a painting was commissioned. The dramatic red made from a substance called cinnabar, which was used to paint wall panels in Pompeii, Italy, around 60 BC, was mined in Spain and was so expensive that a law was passed setting a maximum price. Another colour, ultramarine blue, was extracted from lapis lazuli stone, which had to be quarried and shipped from Afghanistan. Because of the expense, in western art ultramarine blue was reserved for paintings of very special or important people.\n\nF  A new technique in the early 15th century increased the range of colours that could be shown with oil paint. In this technique, the powdered pigment is mixed with a slow-drying oil, such as linseed or walnut oil, which absorbs oxygen from the air and forms a transparent skin that locks the colour in. This meant that oil paint could be built up in layers, so only three or four pigments could be used to create over 20 different shades of, say, red. In the 19th century, the invention of collapsible tin paint tubes to replace pigs' bladders made paint much more portable.\n\nG  So where will artists get their colour inspiration from next? Perhaps more metallic or fluorescent colours will be used, or perhaps artists will use liquid crystals that change colour with temperature or show an iridescent rainbow all at once. One thing is certain: technology will always open new doors for artists."
